1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,868
You're in space.

2
00:00:01,888 --> 00:00:03,067
No helmet.

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:04,231
No suit.

4
00:00:04,251 --> 00:00:05,997
You have about fifteen seconds.

5
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:06,671
Let's count them.

6
00:00:08,021 --> 00:00:09,913
First, forget the movies.

7
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:10,892
Exploding heads,

8
00:00:10,892 --> 00:00:12,553
instant ice statues:

9
00:00:12,573 --> 00:00:13,520
that's Hollywood.

10
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:14,999
You would not explode.

11
00:00:15,019 --> 00:00:16,032
And you would not freeze

12
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:17,011
into an ice cube in

13
00:00:17,011 --> 00:00:18,107
a second, either.

14
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:19,479
The real danger is quieter.

15
00:00:22,479 --> 00:00:23,807
Here's why.

16
00:00:23,827 --> 00:00:24,657
At the beach,

17
00:00:24,677 --> 00:00:25,869
the air above you presses

18
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:27,538
on you with about 101

19
00:00:27,538 --> 00:00:29,256
kilopascals of pressure.

20
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:30,416
You never notice it,

21
00:00:30,436 --> 00:00:31,616
but your body is built

22
00:00:31,616 --> 00:00:32,265
for it.

23
00:00:32,285 --> 00:00:32,555
In space,

24
00:00:32,575 --> 00:00:35,515
that number is practically zero.

25
00:00:35,535 --> 00:00:37,362
The real enemy is oxygen.

26
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:38,362
Or rather,

27
00:00:38,382 --> 00:00:39,228
the lack of it.

28
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:40,368
In a near vacuum,

29
00:00:40,388 --> 00:00:41,816
there's no air pushing oxygen

30
00:00:41,816 --> 00:00:42,781
into your blood.

31
00:00:42,801 --> 00:00:43,929
You'd have about ten to

32
00:00:43,929 --> 00:00:45,949
fifteen seconds of useful consciousness.

33
00:00:45,969 --> 00:00:46,912
Then you'd pass out.

34
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:49,612
And one important rule:

35
00:00:49,632 --> 00:00:50,767
don't hold your breath.

36
00:00:50,787 --> 00:00:51,701
The air in your lungs

37
00:00:51,701 --> 00:00:53,476
would expand and could damage them.

38
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:54,698
So you'd want to breathe out.

39
00:00:55,699 --> 00:00:57,606
Next, something strange.

40
00:00:57,626 --> 00:00:58,976
With almost no pressure,

41
00:00:58,976 --> 00:01:00,989
water boils at body temperature.

42
00:01:01,009 --> 00:01:02,023
So the moisture on your

43
00:01:02,023 --> 00:01:02,927
tongue and in your eyes

44
00:01:02,927 --> 00:01:04,142
would start to bubble.

45
00:01:04,162 --> 00:01:05,660
Your blood is safe, though.

46
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:07,009
Your own blood pressure keeps

47
00:01:07,009 --> 00:01:07,790
it from boiling.

48
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:09,968
This already happens high in

49
00:01:09,968 --> 00:01:10,619
our own sky,

50
00:01:10,619 --> 00:01:12,263
at about nineteen kilometres up.

51
00:01:12,283 --> 00:01:14,174
Pilots call it the Armstrong limit.

52
00:01:14,194 --> 00:01:16,154
Above it, without a pressure suit,

53
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:18,466
the same bubbling starts.

54
00:01:18,486 --> 00:01:19,635
Those bubbles make your body

55
00:01:19,635 --> 00:01:20,256
swell up,

56
00:01:20,276 --> 00:01:21,580
maybe to about twice its

57
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:22,483
normal size.

58
00:01:22,503 --> 00:01:24,380
Doctors call it ebullism.

59
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:25,753
But your skin is strong.

60
00:01:25,773 --> 00:01:27,464
It holds you together.

61
00:01:27,484 --> 00:01:28,173
No popping.

62
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:30,768
It really happened to a

63
00:01:30,768 --> 00:01:33,066
pilot in 1960.

64
00:01:33,086 --> 00:01:34,516
During a record balloon flight

65
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:35,770
more than thirty kilometres

66
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:37,202
up, his glove lost pressure.

67
00:01:37,222 --> 00:01:38,550
His hand swelled to about

68
00:01:38,550 --> 00:01:39,592
twice its size.

69
00:01:39,612 --> 00:01:40,792
He still made his jump,

70
00:01:40,812 --> 00:01:41,877
and a few hours after

71
00:01:41,877 --> 00:01:43,358
landing, his hand was back

72
00:01:43,358 --> 00:01:43,907
to normal.

73
00:01:44,907 --> 00:01:46,240
And here's a true story.

74
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:48,660
In December 1966,

75
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:50,880
at NASA's centre in Houston,

76
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:52,328
a suit tester named Jim

77
00:01:52,328 --> 00:01:55,006
LeBlanc was inside a vacuum chamber.

78
00:01:55,026 --> 00:01:56,776
Suddenly, a hose came loose,

79
00:01:56,796 --> 00:01:58,034
and his suit lost almost

80
00:01:58,034 --> 00:01:59,698
all its pressure.

81
00:01:59,718 --> 00:02:00,805
He later said he felt

82
00:02:00,805 --> 00:02:01,891
the saliva on his tongue

83
00:02:01,891 --> 00:02:02,931
start to bubble.

84
00:02:02,951 --> 00:02:04,224
He passed out after about

85
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:05,322
fourteen seconds.

86
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:06,355
Normally, refilling that

87
00:02:06,355 --> 00:02:08,026
chamber took about half an hour.

88
00:02:08,046 --> 00:02:08,716
That day,

89
00:02:08,736 --> 00:02:09,660
his team flooded it with

90
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:10,856
air in about a minute,

91
00:02:10,876 --> 00:02:11,813
and he recovered.

92
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:13,091
His main complaint?

93
00:02:13,111 --> 00:02:13,829
Sore ears.

94
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,061
So how long would rescuers have?

95
00:02:17,081 --> 00:02:19,325
Experiments in the 1960s found

96
00:02:19,325 --> 00:02:20,755
that animals usually recovered

97
00:02:20,755 --> 00:02:22,125
from about ninety seconds of

98
00:02:22,125 --> 00:02:23,031
near vacuum,

99
00:02:23,051 --> 00:02:24,780
sometimes a bit more.

100
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,586
Much longer was often deadly.

101
00:02:26,606 --> 00:02:27,700
Scientists think a short

102
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:29,052
exposure, under about thirty

103
00:02:29,052 --> 00:02:30,810
seconds, may cause no lasting

104
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:32,465
harm at all.

105
00:02:32,485 --> 00:02:33,374
And the cold?

106
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:35,031
Space works like a thermos.

107
00:02:35,051 --> 00:02:36,097
With no air to carry

108
00:02:36,097 --> 00:02:36,911
heat away,

109
00:02:36,931 --> 00:02:38,334
you'd cool down slowly.

110
00:02:38,354 --> 00:02:39,916
The bigger problem is the Sun.

111
00:02:39,936 --> 00:02:41,109
Without our air and ozone

112
00:02:41,109 --> 00:02:42,266
layer to filter it,

113
00:02:42,286 --> 00:02:43,513
its ultraviolet light would

114
00:02:43,513 --> 00:02:44,683
give you a sunburn far

115
00:02:44,683 --> 00:02:46,833
faster than on any beach.

116
00:02:46,853 --> 00:02:48,208
This is why astronauts take

117
00:02:48,208 --> 00:02:49,273
it so seriously.

118
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:51,103
In 1971,

119
00:02:51,123 --> 00:02:53,098
three cosmonauts on Soyuz 11

120
00:02:53,098 --> 00:02:54,598
died when a valve opened

121
00:02:54,598 --> 00:02:57,463
about 168 kilometres up,

122
00:02:57,483 --> 00:02:58,392
letting the air out of

123
00:02:58,392 --> 00:02:59,556
their capsule.

124
00:02:59,576 --> 00:03:00,768
They are the only people

125
00:03:00,768 --> 00:03:02,181
ever to die in space.

126
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:03,251
Since then,

127
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:04,595
crews wear pressure suits for

128
00:03:04,595 --> 00:03:06,463
launch and landing.

129
00:03:06,483 --> 00:03:07,973
And on the Space Station,

130
00:03:07,993 --> 00:03:08,996
the cabin is kept at

131
00:03:08,996 --> 00:03:10,203
about the same air pressure

132
00:03:10,203 --> 00:03:11,246
as at sea level here

133
00:03:11,246 --> 00:03:11,916
on Earth.

134
00:03:11,936 --> 00:03:13,259
Which means astronauts can

135
00:03:13,259 --> 00:03:14,421
work in T-shirts.

136
00:03:14,441 --> 00:03:15,937
The station's walls hold that

137
00:03:15,937 --> 00:03:16,631
air in,

138
00:03:16,651 --> 00:03:18,203
like a giant flying bottle.

139
00:03:18,223 --> 00:03:20,101
Outside, it's vacuum.

140
00:03:20,121 --> 00:03:21,149
Inside, it's home.

141
00:03:22,498 --> 00:03:23,762
So: about fifteen seconds

142
00:03:23,762 --> 00:03:25,324
awake, about ninety seconds

143
00:03:25,324 --> 00:03:26,268
for a rescue,

144
00:03:26,288 --> 00:03:27,517
and no explosions.

145
00:03:27,537 --> 00:03:28,867
Space is dangerous,

146
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:29,699
but not in the way

147
00:03:29,699 --> 00:03:30,397
movies show.

148
00:03:30,417 --> 00:03:32,107
The real killer is simple:

149
00:03:32,127 --> 00:03:33,430
no air to breathe.

150
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:35,324
That's why every spacesuit is

151
00:03:35,324 --> 00:03:36,530
really a tiny,

152
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:37,217
personal spaceship.

153
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:37,597
Next time,

154
00:03:37,617 --> 00:03:39,427
we go somewhere far worse:

155
00:03:39,447 --> 00:03:40,175
a black hole.

156
00:03:41,576 --> 00:03:42,639
How many seconds do you

157
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:43,577
think you'd last?

158
00:03:43,597 --> 00:03:44,830
Tell me in the comments.

159
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:46,900
Next time, we fall even farther:

160
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:48,292
straight into a black hole.

161
00:03:48,312 --> 00:03:49,924
Subscribe, and hold on to

162
00:03:49,924 --> 00:03:50,432
your spaghetti.
